427. Why Page Object Model?
Interview answer

I use POM to separate test logic from UI implementation.

If a locator changes, I update it in one page class instead of modifying multiple test cases.

It improves maintainability, readability, reusability, and reduces code duplication.

Example:

Without POM:

driver.findElement(By.id("loginBtn")).click();

appearing in 50 tests.

With POM:

loginPage.clickLogin();

If the locator changes, only LoginPage needs modification.

428. What is POM?

POM = Page Object Model.

It is a design pattern where each application page/component is represented by a Java class.

Example:

public class LoginPage {

    private WebDriver driver;

    private By username = By.id("username");
    private By password = By.id("password");
    private By loginButton = By.id("login");

    public LoginPage(WebDriver driver) {
        this.driver = driver;
    }

    public void login(String user, String pass) {
        driver.findElement(username).sendKeys(user);
        driver.findElement(password).sendKeys(pass);
        driver.findElement(loginButton).click();
    }
}

Test:

@Test
public void loginTest() {

    LoginPage loginPage = new LoginPage(driver);

    loginPage.login("admin", "password");
}
429. POM vs PageFactory?
POM	PageFactory
Design pattern	Selenium support mechanism
Organizes page classes	Initializes WebElements
Can use By locators	Usually uses @FindBy
Does not require PageFactory	Often used with POM
Modern frameworks can avoid PageFactory	Less preferred in many modern frameworks
Example PageFactory
@FindBy(id = "username")
WebElement username;

Then:

PageFactory.initElements(driver, this);
Strong answer

POM is the design pattern. PageFactory is a Selenium mechanism that can be used to initialize elements inside a page object. They are not alternatives. We can implement POM without PageFactory, and in many modern frameworks I prefer explicit locators with By and reusable wait methods.

430. What is BaseTest?

BaseTest contains common test setup and teardown.

Example:

public class BaseTest {

    protected WebDriver driver;

    @BeforeMethod
    public void setUp() {

        driver = DriverFactory.initDriver("chrome");

        driver.get(ConfigReader.getUrl());
    }

    @AfterMethod
    public void tearDown() {

        if (driver != null) {
            driver.quit();
        }
    }
}

Then:

public class LoginTest extends BaseTest {

    @Test
    public void loginTest() {

        LoginPage page = new LoginPage(driver);

        page.login("admin", "password");
    }
}
Purpose

Avoid repeating:

browser setup
URL
driver initialization
cleanup

in every test.

431. What is BasePage?

BasePage contains common Selenium operations.

For example:

public class BasePage {

    protected WebDriver driver;

    public BasePage(WebDriver driver) {
        this.driver = driver;
    }

    public void click(By locator) {
        driver.findElement(locator).click();
    }

    public void type(By locator, String text) {
        driver.findElement(locator).sendKeys(text);
    }
}

Page:

public class LoginPage extends BasePage {

    private By username = By.id("username");

    public LoginPage(WebDriver driver) {
        super(driver);
    }

    public void enterUsername(String value) {
        type(username, value);
    }
}
Difference

BaseTest

→ manages test lifecycle.

BasePage

→ manages common UI operations.

432. What is Driver Factory?

DriverFactory is responsible for creating and managing WebDriver instances.

Example:

public class DriverFactory {

    public static WebDriver createDriver(String browser) {

        if (browser.equalsIgnoreCase("chrome")) {
            return new ChromeDriver();
        }

        if (browser.equalsIgnoreCase("firefox")) {
            return new FirefoxDriver();
        }

        throw new RuntimeException("Invalid browser");
    }
}

Then:

driver = DriverFactory.createDriver("chrome");
Why?

Instead of creating drivers everywhere:

new ChromeDriver();

we centralize driver creation.

